)\n-   [Add to Search](#)\n\n. 2013 Feb;65(2):418-28.\n\ndoi: 10.1002/art.37760.\n\n# Zone-specific gene expression patterns in articular cartilage\n\n[Shawn P Grogan](/?term=Grogan+SP&cauthor_id=23124445)  [1](#full-view-affiliation-1 \"The Scripps Research Institute and Shiley Center for Orthopaedic Research and Education, Scripps Clinic, La Jolla, California, USA.\") , [Stuart F Duffy](/?term=Duffy+SF&cauthor_id=23124445), [Chantal Pauli](/?term=Pauli+C&cauthor_id=23124445), [James A Koziol](/?term=Koziol+JA&cauthor_id=23124445), [Andrew I Su](/?term=Su+AI&cauthor_id=23124445), [Darryl D D'Lima](/?term=D%27Lima+DD&cauthor_id=23124445), [Martin K Lotz](/?term=Lotz+MK&cauthor_id=23124445)\n\nAffiliations Expand\n\n### Affiliation\n\n-   1 The Scripps Research Institute and Shiley Center for Orthopaedic Research and Education, Scripps Clinic, La Jolla, California, USA.\n\n-   PMID: **23124445**\n-   PMCID: [PMC3558601](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3558601/)\n-   DOI: [10.1002/art.37760](https://doi.org/10.1002/art.37760)\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\n# Zone-specific gene expression patterns in articular cartilage\n\nShawn P Grogan et al. Arthritis Rheum. 2013 Feb.\n\nShow details\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat Abstract PubMed PMID\n\nArthritis Rheum\n\nActions\n\n-   [Search in PubMed](/?term=%22Arthritis+Rheum%22%5Bjour%5D&sort=date&sort_order=desc)\n-   [Search in NLM Catalog](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/nlmcatalog?term=%22Arthritis+Rheum%22%5BTitle+Abbreviation%5D)\n-   [Add to Search](#)\n\n. 2013 Feb;65(2):418-28.\n\ndoi: 10.1002/art.37760.\n\n### Authors\n\n[Shawn P Grogan](/?term=Grogan+SP&cauthor_id=23124445)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"The Scripps Research Institute and Shiley Center for Orthopaedic Research and Education, Scripps Clinic, La Jolla, California, USA.\") , [Stuart F Duffy](/?term=Duffy+SF&cauthor_id=23124445), [Chantal Pauli](/?term=Pauli+C&cauthor_id=23124445), [James A Koziol](/?term=Koziol+JA&cauthor_id=23124445), [Andrew I Su](/?term=Su+AI&cauthor_id=23124445), [Darryl D D'Lima](/?term=D%27Lima+DD&cauthor_id=23124445), [Martin K Lotz](/?term=Lotz+MK&cauthor_id=23124445)\n\n### Affiliation\n\n-   1 The Scripps Research Institute and Shiley Center for Orthopaedic Research and Education, Scripps Clinic, La Jolla, California, USA.\n\n-   PMID: **23124445**\n-   PMCID: [PMC3558601](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3558601/)\n-   DOI: [10.1002/art.37760](https://doi.org/10.1002/art.37760)\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\nFull text links Cite\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat AbstractPubMedPMID\n\n## Abstract\n\n**Objective:** To identify novel genes and pathways specific to the superficial zone (SZ), middle zone (MZ), and deep zone (DZ) of normal articular cartilage.\n\n**Methods:** Articular cartilage was obtained from the knees of 4 normal human donors. The cartilage zones were dissected on a microtome. RNA was analyzed on human genome arrays. The zone-specific DNA array data obtained from human tissue were compared to array data obtained from bovine cartilage. Genes differentially expressed between zones were evaluated using direct annotation for structural or functional features, and by enrichment analysis for integrated pathways or functions.\n\n**Results:** The greatest differences in genome-wide RNA expression data were between the SZ and DZ in both human and bovine cartilage. The MZ, being a transitional zone between the SZ and DZ, thereby shared some of the same pathways as well as structural/functional features of the adjacent zones. Cellular functions and biologic processes that were enriched in the SZ relative to the DZ included, most prominently, extracellular matrix-receptor interactions, cell adhesion molecule functions, regulation of actin cytoskeleton, ribosome-related functions, and signaling aspects such as the IFN, IL4, Cdc42/Rac, and JAK/STAT signaling pathways. Two pathways were enriched in the DZ relative to the SZ, including PPARG and EGFR/SMRTE.\n\n**Conclusion:** These differences in cartilage zonal gene expression identify new markers and pathways that govern the unique differentiation status of chondrocyte subpopulations.\n\nCopyright © 2013 by the American College of Rheumatology.\n\n[PubMed Disclaimer](/disclaimer/)\n\n## Figures\n\n [![Figure 1](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/5c5f/3558601/04c5d7fcd69c/nihms417098f1.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/5c5f/3558601/83bccc6bdbd5/nihms417098f1.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 1\n\n**\n\nGenes that are differentially expressed…\n\n**\n\nFigure 1\n\n**\n\nGenes that are differentially expressed in a zone-specific manner in human cartilage, which…\n\n**Figure 1**\n\nGenes that are differentially expressed in a zone-specific manner in human cartilage, which were also cross-validated in the bovine data set. Shown are the donor-wise target-normalized signal intensities for the 24 human cartilage zone-specific genes that are significantly different across the 3 zones of the 4 normal human donors (A, B, C, and D).\n\n [![Figure 2](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/5c5f/3558601/7e2b01938183/nihms417098f2.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/5c5f/3558601/6e0e3043a0c2/nihms417098f2.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 2\n\n**\n\nPathway analysis A. Significantly enriched…\n\n**\n\nFigure 2\n\n**\n\nPathway analysis A. Significantly enriched KEGG pathways in the superficial zone (SZ) compared…\n\n**Figure 2**\n\nPathway analysis A. Significantly enriched KEGG pathways in the superficial zone (SZ) compared to the DZ following GSEA (gene set enrichment analysis). B. Visual representation of the relationship between genes significantly enriched GSEA pathways for the superficial zone using STRING network analysis. Combined view of KEGG pathways for the SZ, including ECM receptor interaction, cell adhesion molecules and regulation of actin cytoskeleton.\n\n [![Figure 3](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/5c5f/3558601/5529349e9b79/nihms417098f3.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/5c5f/3558601/2cc5e3d7da27/nihms417098f3.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 3\n\n**\n\nHeat map of all differentially…\n\n**\n\nFigure 3\n\n**\n\nHeat map of all differentially expressed genes in each zone of human articular…\n\n**Figure 3**\n\nHeat map of all differentially expressed genes in each zone of human articular cartilage (donors A–D).\n\n[See this image and copyright information in PMC](#)\n\n## References\n\n1.  1.  Poole AR, Kojima T, Yasuda T, Mwale F, Kobayashi M, Laverty S. Composition and structure of articular cartilage: a template for tissue repair. Clin Orthop Relat Res. 2001:S26–33. - [PubMed](/11603710/)\n2.  1.  Camacho NP, West P, Torzilli PA, Mendelsohn R. FTIR microscopic imaging of collagen and proteoglycan in bovine cartilage. Biopolymers. 2001;62:1–8. - [PubMed](/11135186/)\n3.  1.  Muir H, Bullough P, Maroudas A. The distribution of collagen in human articular cartilage with some of its physiological implications. J Bone Joint Surg Br. 1970;52:554–63. - [PubMed](/4247851/)\n4.  1.  Venn MF. Variation of chemical composition with age in human femoral head cartilage. Ann Rheum Dis. 1978;37:168–74. - [PMC](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1001184/) - [PubMed](/148244/)\n5.  1.  Poole AR, Rosenberg LC, Reiner A, Ionescu M, Bogoch E, Roughley PJ. Contents and distributions of the proteoglycans decorin and biglycan in normal and osteoarthritic human articular cartilage. J Orthop Res. 1996;14:681–9. - [PubMed](/8893759/)\n\nShow all 56 references\n\n## Publication types\n\n-   Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural\n    \n    Actions\n    \n    -   [Search in PubMed](/?term=%22Research+Support%2C+N.I.H.%2C+Extramural%22%5Bpt%5D&sort=date&sort_order=desc)\n    -   [Search in MeSH](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/mesh?term=Research+Support%2C+N.I.H.%2C+Extramural)\n    -   [Add to Search](#)\n    \n-   Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't\n    \n    Actions\n    \n    -   [Search in PubMed](/?term=%22Research+Support%2C+Non-U.S.+