d\n\n# Histological and Immunohistochemical Methods in Normal and Osteoarthritic Knee Cartilage of Rat and Rabbit Models: A Literature Review\n\nAna Sabucedo-Suárez et al. Int J Mol Sci. 2025.\n\nShow details\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat Abstract PubMed PMID\n\nInt J Mol Sci\n\nActions\n\n-   [Search in PubMed](/?term=%22Int+J+Mol+Sci%22%5Bjour%5D&sort=date&sort_order=desc)\n-   [Search in NLM Catalog](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/nlmcatalog?term=%22Int+J+Mol+Sci%22%5BTitle+Abbreviation%5D)\n-   [Add to Search](#)\n\n. 2025 Oct 23;26(21):10300.\n\ndoi: 10.3390/ijms262110300.\n\n### Authors\n\n[Ana Sabucedo-Suárez](/?term=Sabucedo-Su%C3%A1rez+A&cauthor_id=41226342)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Anatomy, Animal Production and Veterinary Clinical Sciences Department, Veterinary Faculty, Universidade de Santiago de Compostela, 27002 Lugo, Spain.\")   [2](#short-view-affiliation-2 \"Laboratory of Biomaterials, Ibonelab S.L., 27004 Lugo, Spain.\") , [María Permuy](/?term=Permuy+M&cauthor_id=41226342)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Anatomy, Animal Production and Veterinary Clinical Sciences Department, Veterinary Faculty, Universidade de Santiago de Compostela, 27002 Lugo, Spain.\") , [Fernando Muñoz](/?term=Mu%C3%B1oz+F&cauthor_id=41226342)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Anatomy, Animal Production and Veterinary Clinical Sciences Department, Veterinary Faculty, Universidade de Santiago de Compostela, 27002 Lugo, Spain.\") , [Mónica López-Peña](/?term=L%C3%B3pez-Pe%C3%B1a+M&cauthor_id=41226342)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Anatomy, Animal Production and Veterinary Clinical Sciences Department, Veterinary Faculty, Universidade de Santiago de Compostela, 27002 Lugo, Spain.\")\n\n### Affiliations\n\n-   1 Anatomy, Animal Production and Veterinary Clinical Sciences Department, Veterinary Faculty, Universidade de Santiago de Compostela, 27002 Lugo, Spain.\n-   2 Laboratory of Biomaterials, Ibonelab S.L., 27004 Lugo, Spain.\n\n-   PMID: **41226342**\n-   PMCID: [PMC12609312](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12609312/)\n-   DOI: [10.3390/ijms262110300](https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms262110300)\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\nFull text links Cite\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat AbstractPubMedPMID\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe tissue covering the bones in synovial joints is called articular cartilage. Chondrocytes produce and maintain the extracellular matrix and, based on their shape and the orientation of the collagen fibers, articular cartilage is separated into four histological zones: superficial, middle, deep, and calcified zones. Osteoarthritis is a degenerative joint disorder in which mechanical, biochemical, and inflammatory factors contribute to the disruption of the balance between extracellular matrix synthesis and degradation. This article aims to review the literature published to date by identifying the techniques most used in immunohistochemistry and histology for the detection and grading of knee osteoarthritis in rabbit/rat models. A systematic review was carried out using databases to find publications that assessed osteoarthritis in rabbit/rat knee models using histological and immunohistochemical methods. Out of 766 initial articles, 56 met the criteria. Hematoxylin-Eosin and Safranin O demonstrated clear distinctions between healthy and osteoarthritis cartilage. Immunohistochemical findings showed decreased expression of type II collagen and increased expression of matrix metalloproteinases and caspase-3 in osteoarthritis cartilage. Although both have limitations, histology stains are useful for evaluating cartilage structure and osteoarthritis progression. On the other hand, immunohistochemistry techniques support established osteoarthritis processes, including apoptosis, metalloproteinases activity, and collagen degradation. Future research should explore additional pathways to improve osteoarthritis understanding.\n\n**Keywords:** articular cartilage; histology; immunohistochemistry; osteoarthritis; rabbit; rat.\n\n[PubMed Disclaimer](/disclaimer/)\n\n## Conflict of interest statement\n\nAuthor Ana Sabucedo-Suárez was employed by the company IboneLab S.L. The remaining authors declare that the research was conducted in the absence of any commercial or financial relationships that could be construed as a potential conflict of interest.\n\n## Figures\n\n [![Figure 1](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/b7b8/12609312/20e0889ae09d/ijms-26-10300-g001.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/b7b8/12609312/e99d73f57768/ijms-26-10300-g001.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 1\n\n**\n\nLayers of the normal articular…\n\n**\n\nFigure 1\n\n**\n\nLayers of the normal articular cartilage. SZ—superficial zone; MZ—middle zone; DZ—deep zone; CC—calcified…\n\n**Figure 1**\n\nLayers of the normal articular cartilage. SZ—superficial zone; MZ—middle zone; DZ—deep zone; CC—calcified cartilage; SB—subchondral bone.\n\n [![Figure 2](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/b7b8/12609312/989407b25cd7/ijms-26-10300-g002.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/b7b8/12609312/371b6dca5a79/ijms-26-10300-g002.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 2\n\n**\n\nChanges in the articular cartilage…\n\n**\n\nFigure 2\n\n**\n\nChanges in the articular cartilage in the presence of OA. SZ—superficial zone; MZ—middle…\n\n**Figure 2**\n\nChanges in the articular cartilage in the presence of OA. SZ—superficial zone; MZ—middle zone; DZ—deep zone; CC—calcified cartilage; SB—subchondral bone. Progressive structural disorganization, loss of chondrocytes, and disruption of the collagen fiber network, together with subchondral bone thickening and sclerosis can be observed. These histological alterations illustrate the typical zonal progression of osteoarthritic degeneration.\n\n [![Figure 3](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/b7b8/12609312/6d2153529ab5/ijms-26-10300-g003.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/b7b8/12609312/99443169ef3f/ijms-26-10300-g003.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 3\n\n**\n\nFlow chart of the final…\n\n**\n\nFigure 3\n\n**\n\nFlow chart of the final selection.\n\n**Figure 3**\n\nFlow chart of the final selection.\n\n [![Figure 4](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/b7b8/12609312/43594c93675c/ijms-26-10300-g004.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/b7b8/12609312/050a41e05005/ijms-26-10300-g004.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 4\n\n**\n\nPercentage of most commonly histological…\n\n**\n\nFigure 4\n\n**\n\nPercentage of most commonly histological stains used by the authors. Hematoxylin–Eosin (HE) and…\n\n**Figure 4**\n\nPercentage of most commonly histological stains used by the authors. Hematoxylin–Eosin (HE) and Safranin O–Fast Green, representing 40% and 31%, respectively, were the most frequently applied, reflecting their widespread utility for general morphological and glycosaminoglycan assessment, respectively. Toluidine Blue (13%) was also extensively used to evaluate proteoglycan content.\n\n [![Figure 5](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/b7b8/12609312/fea602a1f793/ijms-26-10300-g005.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/b7b8/12609312/3d5c4bcb3fda/ijms-26-10300-g005.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 5\n\n**\n\nPercentage of most used immunohistochemical…\n\n**\n\nFigure 5\n\n**\n\nPercentage of most used immunohistochemical markers in rat and rabbit models of osteoarthritis.…\n\n**Figure 5**\n\nPercentage of most used immunohistochemical markers in rat and rabbit models of osteoarthritis. Collagen type II (COL-II) was the most frequently assessed, reaching 28%, reflecting extracellular matrix integrity, followed by matrix metalloproteinase-13 (MMP-13) (20%), a key enzyme in cartilage degradation. Caspase-3 was used in 7% of the studies to evaluate chondrocyte apoptosis.\n\n [![Figure 6](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/b7b8/12609312/797ee7cf5af3/ijms-26-10300-g006.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/b7b8/12609312/d7bf7280354b/ijms-26-10300-g006.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 6\n\n**\n\nDifference between markers used in…\n\n**\n\nFigure 6\n\n**\n\nDifference between markers used in rabbit studies vs. rat studies included in this…\n\n**Figure 6**\n\nDifference between markers used in rabbit studies vs. rat studies included in this article. In both species, collagen type II (COL-II) was the most frequently assessed. In rats, MMP-13 was the second most analyzed marker, whereas, in rabbits, MMP-1 predominated. Collagen type I (COL-I) and caspase-3 were used more extensively in rabbits than in rats.\n\n[See this image and copyright information in PMC](#)\n\n## References\n\n1.  1.  Martel-Pelletier J., Boileau C., Pelletier J.-P., Roughley P.J. Cartilage in Normal and Osteoarthritis Conditions. Best Pract. Res. Clin. Rheumatol. 2008;22:351–384. doi: 10.1016/j.berh.2008.02.001. - [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.berh.2008.02.001) - [PubMed](/18455690/)\n2.  1.  Tian Y., Peng Z., Gorton D., Xiao Y., Ketheesan N. Immunohistochemical Analysis of Structural Changes in Collagen for the Assessment of Osteoarthritis. Proc. Inst. Mech. Eng. Part H J. Eng. Med. 2011;225:680–687. doi: 10.1177/0954411911402135. - [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1177/0954411911402135) - [PubMed](/21870375/)\n3.  1.  Vaca-González J.J., Gutiérrez M.L., Garzón-Alvarado D.A. Cartílago articular: Estructura, patologías y campos eléctricos como alternativa terapéutica. Revisión de conceptos actuales. Rev. Colomb. Ortop. Traumatol. 2017;31:202–210. doi: 10.1016/j.rccot.2017.06.002. - [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rccot.2017.06.002)\n4.  1.  Erggelet C., Mandelbaum B.R., Mrosek E.H., Scopp J.M. Principles of Cartilage Repair. Steinkopff; Heidelberg, Germany: 2008. p. 121. - [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-7985-1593-2)\n5.  1.  Carballo C.B., Nakagawa Y., Sekiya I., Rodeo S.A. Basic Science of Articular Cartilage. Clin. Sports Med. 2017;36:413–425. doi: 10.1016/j.csm.2017.02.001. - [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.csm.2017.02.001) - [PubMed](/28577703/)\n\nShow all 91 references\n\n## Publication types\n\n-   Systematic Review\n    \n    Actions\n